# Untitled

Evidence for Declining Extracellular Calcium Uptake and Protein Kinase C Activity in Uterine Arterial Smooth Muscle during Gestation in Gilts<sup>1</sup>
D. B. FARLEY<sup>3</sup> and S. P. FORD<sup>2,4</sup>
Department of Ob/Gyn,<sup>3</sup> University of Iowa, Iowa City, Iowa 52242  Department of Animal Science,<sup>4</sup> Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa 50011
## ABSTRACT
Uterine arterial blood flow and uterine arterial diameter are known to increase dramatically and progressively throughout gestation. Previous data from our laboratory have demonstrated that the KCl-induced membrane depolarization of uterine arterial smooth muscle specifically induces \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake through the potentially sensitive channels (PSC). Evidence from other laboratories suggests that calcium uptake through the PSC mediates long-term changes in uterine arterial diameter and flow (tone), possibly through activation of protein kinase C (PKC). In study 1 we evaluated uterine arteries removed from gilts on Days 20, 50, 80, and 110 of gestation for their ability to take up extracellular \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) and to contract in response to a depolarizing dose of KCl. The ability of KCl to induce contraction of uterine arteries as well as its ability to stimulate extracellular \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake by these same arteries declines \((p<0.01)\) progressively from Day 20 through Day 110 of gestation. Estrogen concentrations in systemic blood were negatively correlated with the contractile response \((r=-0.57; p<0.01)\) and extracellular \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake \((r=-0.93; p<0.0001)\) of uterine arteries during gestation. In study 2 we evaluated changes in uterine arterial PKC and protein kinase M (PKM) throughout the estrous cycle and gestation. It was determined that cytosolic PKC declined with the advancement of gestation whereas PKM progressively increased \((r=-0.63; p<0.01)\). These data suggest a decreasing ability of the uterine artery to take up extracellular \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) through the PSC as gestation advances, in association with decreasing cytosolic PKC.
## INTRODUCTION
Uterine blood flow increases progressively with the advancement of gestation in the pig, in association with increasing production of placental estrogen [1]. Although corresponding increases in uterine arterial diameter also are observed, the uterine arterial vasculature retains marked sensitivity to short-term (5-10 min) contractile effects of circulating catecholamines [2-5]. Vascular smooth muscle contraction requires the uptake of extracellular \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) [6]. Calcium enters uterine arterial smooth muscle cells predominantly through two specific membrane channels: 1) receptor-operated channels (ROC) specifically activated by \(\alpha_{1}\)-adrenergic receptor agonists and 2) potential-sensitive channels (PSC) specifically activated by membrane depolarization [7,8]. Activation of ROC by catecholamines during maternal stress facilitates short-term reductions in uterine arterial diameter and flow. Previous data from our laboratory [5] demonstrated that \(\alpha_{1}\)-adrenergic receptor numbers and phenylephrine-induced contraction of in vitro-perfused uterine arteries remain constant throughout gestation in pigs. The activation of PSC results in prolonged decreases in vessel diameter and flow [9]. Because uterine arterial diameter and flow increases progressively with the
advancement of gestation, a selective reduction in \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake through the PSC is indicated.
Recent evidence emphasizes participation of \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\)-activated, phospholipid-dependent protein kinase (PKC) as an important intracellular mediator of the sustained phase of contractile responses of vascular smooth muscle [10, 11]. Furthermore, the proteolytic activation of PKC results in formation of a soluble \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\)/lipid-independent kinase (PKM), which also may have functional significance in coordinating cellular responses such as protein phosphorylation [12-14]. Furthermore, Magness et al. [15] demonstrated that increased estrogen production during the estrous cycle and late gestation in ewes is associated with significant declines in uterine but not systemic arterial PKC activity.
Our overall hypothesis was that \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake through the PSC mediates long-term changes in uterine arterial diameter by modulating the activity of PKC. Study 1 was conducted to evaluate changes in uterine arterial contractile properties and specific \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptakes in response to KCl-induced membrane depolarization throughout gestation in gilts. Study 2 was conducted to quantify the PKC and PKM activity of uterine arterial smooth muscle during the estrous cycle and the first, second, and third trimesters of gestation in gilts, and to relate these changes to changes in PSC activity observed in Study 1.
## MATERIALS AND METHODS
## Study 1
Animals: Yorkshire gilts (10-12 mo; 140-160 kg) were mated to a boar and assigned randomly to slaughter on Days 20 (n = 6), 50 (n = 6), 80 (n = 4), or 110 (n = 6)
of gestation. All gilts were mated on the first day of behavioral estrus (Day 0). Immediately before slaughter, a sample of blood was obtained from the anterior vena cava of each gilt, and the plasma was frozen at \(-20^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) until quantification of steroid concentration by RIA.
At slaughter, two adjacent \(3.5\mathrm{-cm}\) segments of the middle uterine artery supplying one uterine horn were excised immediately proximal to its first bifurcation in the mesometrium. One section was placed into a container of oxygenated \((95\%\ \mathrm{O}_{2};5\%\ \mathrm{CO}_{2})\) Krebs-Ringer solution \((22^{\circ}\mathbf{C};\) millimolar composition \(=\) NaCl, 118.1; \(\mathbf{NaHCO_{3}}\) , 25.0; KCl, 4.7; \(\mathbf{CaCl}_{2}.6\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\) , 2.5; \(\mathbf{KH}_{2}\mathbf{PO}_{4}\) , 1.2; \(\mathbf{MgSO_{4}\cdot7H_{2}O}\) , 1.2; D-glucose, 11.1) for in vitro perfusion. The other segment was placed into a physiological salt solution (PSS; \(22^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) ; millimolar composition \(=\) NaCl, 140.0; \(\mathbf{CaCl}_{2}\) , 1.5; \(\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}\) , 1.0; KCl, 4.5, D-glucose, 10.0; Hepes, 5.0) for \(^{45}\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake measurements. The \(\mathbf{pH}\) of both solutions was adjusted to 7.2 and both were continuously oxygenated with \(95\%\ \mathrm{O}_{2};5\%\) \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) . In addition, two \(3.5\mathrm{-cm}\) sections of the gastroepiploic artery were removed from each gilt and subjected to in vitro perfusion and \(^{45}\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake. The gastroepiploic artery was chosen as a control vessel due to the absence of side branches and its diameter, which is similar to that of the middle uterine artery.
In vitro vessel perfusion. At the laboratory, a uterine arterial segment and a gastroepiploic arterial segment from each animal were prepared for simultaneous perfusion as previously described [16]. Arterial segments were continually perfused both intraluminally and extraluminally with continuously oxygenated Krebs-Ringer solution \((37^{\circ}\mathbf{C})\) . The extraluminal flow rate was maintained at \(20\ \mathrm{ml/min}\) . The intraluminal perfusion rate was held constant at \(40\ \mathrm{ml/min}\) throughout the perfusion of each artery, and a resistance was applied to the arterial outflow to adjust each artery's intraluminal pressure to \(20\ \mathrm{mm\Hg}\) . Arterial segments were allowed a 30-min equilibration period, during which time all arteries established a baseline perfusion pressure (BPP) of approximately \(20\ \mathrm{mm\Hg}\) , which remained constant for the duration of perfusion. We have previously documented that there is a progressive decline in collagen content in the uterine arterial wall throughout pregnancy in the pig with no change in elastin content [5]. Wolensky and Glagov [17] demonstrated that at a BPP well below diastolic values (i.e., \(20\ \mathrm{mm\Hg})\) , the passive tension of the arterial wall depends solely on its elastin content, due to elastin's relatively low modulus of elasticity. Thus, we believe that at this low BPP the changes in collagen content are prevented from obscuring vascular smooth muscle contractile responses.
After equilibration, both arterial segments were perfused intraluminally with KCl at a final concentration of \(200\ \mathrm{mM}\) . The dose of KCl used was determined from a previous study [7] to be in the middle of its dose-response curve for increasing contractions of uterine and gastroepiploic arteries from pigs. The maximal increase in perfusion pressure above
BPP in response to a 5-min exposure to KCl was measured by use of pressure transducers and recorded on a chart recorder. The KCl perfusion was conducted to determine the contractile response of each artery resulting from membrane depolarization. Responses of the arteries to KCl also are an indication of the numbers and/or activity of functional PSCs on the smooth muscle cell membrane. Stice et al. [7] demonstrated that the PSC blocker D-600 (A.G. Knoll Pharmaceutical Laboratories, Ludwigshafen, Germany) specifically and completely inhibits the constrictor response to a depolarizing dose of KCl in uterine arterial segments from pigs.
\(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake studies. Calcium uptake of arterial segments was measured as previously described and validated in this laboratory [7, 8]. Each arterial segment was trimmed of excess connective tissue and cut into four \(0.5\mathrm{-cm}\) segments for \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) determinations. Measurement of unidirectional \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) fluxes were carried out on three of these arterial segments after a 5-min exposure to KCl \((200\ \mathrm{mM})\) and \(0.5\ \mathrm{Ci/ml}\ \mathrm{^{45}Ca^{2+}}\) . The fourth segment was used for estimation of nonspecific binding. This segment was maintained in the presence of \(10^{-3}\ \mathrm{M\LaCl_{3}}\) before and during exposure to \(^{45}\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) and KCl. Nonspecific binding to all arterial segments averaged approximately \(20\%\) of the total \(^{45}\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake for each artery. Nonspecific binding was subtracted from total \(^{45}\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake for each artery to obtain specific uptake. The intraassay coefficient of variation of specific \(^{45}\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake, calculated by running multiple segments \((\mathfrak{n}=6)\) of a luteal phase uterine artery in a single assay, averaged \(6.9\%\) , with an uptake of \(237.6\,+\,5.7\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\) \(^{45}\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}/\mathbf{kg}\) tissue (wet weight).
RIAs. Plasma concentrations of estrone, estradiol-17\(\beta\), and progesterone were quantified by RIA as described and validated previously in this laboratory [18]. For each hormone, all samples were analyzed in a single assay. Sensitivities of the assays, defined as the amount of standard that yielded \(95\%\) \(\mathbf{B}/\mathbf{B_{o}}\), were approximately \(2\ \mathrm{pg}\) for estrone and estradiol-17\(\beta\) and \(50\ \mathrm{pg}\) for progesterone. The intraassay coefficients of variation were 5.4, 9.2, and \(6.5\%\) for estrone, estradiol-17\(\beta\), and progesterone, respectively.
## Study 2
Animals. Uterine arterial tissue were dissected from uteri of pigs determined to be nonpregnant or pregnant at slaughter at a commercial facility. This determination was made by visual observation (mid- to late-gestation) or by flushing each uterine horn with saline (0.15 M NaCl) and examining the flushings for the presence of embryonic tissue (nonpregnant and early gestation). Nonpregnant sows were classified as either in the follicular phase \((\mathfrak{n}=7)\) or luteal phase \((\mathfrak{n}=6)\) based on the ovarian structures observed. Ovaries of follicular phase pigs exhibited large follicles (8-10 mm diameter) and small, white corpora albicantia averaging \(90\pm16\ \mathrm{mg}\) in weight, whereas the ovaries of luteal phase pigs contained large, brick-red, highly vas
cularized corpora lutea (average weight \(=400\pm26~\mathrm{mg}\)) and small follicles (2-4 mm diameter). Pregnant pigs were divided into three groups: 1) Days 35-45; 2) Days 50-75, and 3) Days 80-95 of gestation. Stage of gestation was determined by the observed curved fetal crown-rump length [19].
All branches of the middle uterine artery were dissected from the mesometrium of each uterine horn. Arterial tissue was placed in vials containing sterile \(0.15\mathrm{~M~NaCl}\), immediately frozen on dry ice, and stored at \(-80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) until assayed for PKC and PKM activity within 4 wk of collection.
Preparation of particulate and cytosolic fractions. To facilitate homogenization, vessels were pulverized to a fine powder while frozen in liquid nitrogen and subsequently were homogenized (Brinkman PT 10/35 homogenizer, Westbury, NY) in \(5\;\mathrm{ml}\) of ice-cold \(25~\mathrm{mM}\) Tris-HCl, \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.5\), containing \(0.25\mathrm{~M~}\) sucrose, \(2.5\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\), \(2.5\,\mathrm{\mM}\) EGTA, \(50\,\mathrm{\mM}\) 2-mercaptoethanol, and \(1.0\,\mathrm{\mM}\) PMSF. The tissue homogenate was first centrifuged at \(1\,000\,\times\,g\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\); the supernatant was then collected and centrifuged at \(100\,000\times g\) for \(60\;\mathrm{min}\) at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The resulting supernatant (cytosol) and pellet were retained. The pellet was resuspended in \(5\;\mathrm{ml}\) of homogenization buffer containing \(0.3\%\) Triton X-100 (Sigma, St. Louis, MO). This suspension was pulse-sonicated for 20 sec (Heat Systems-Ultrasonics, Farmingdale, NY; \(40\%\) duty, output level 3, microtip probe), then centrifuged at \(100\,000\times g\) for \(15\ \mathrm{min}\) at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and the supernatant (particulate fraction) was retained. Crude cytosolic and particulate protein determinations were obtained by using the Bio-Rad Protein Assay (Richmond, CA), with BSA as standard.
Partial purification of PKC and PKM by DEAE Bio-Gel A chromatography. Freshly isolated cytosolic and particulate fractions were applied to individual DEAE Bio-Gel A agarose columns \((1\,\times\,3\,\mathrm{cm})\) previously equilibrated with homogenization buffer. The amount of protein added to each column averaged \(8.1\,\pm\,0.3\,\mathrm{mg}\) for cytosolic and \(1.03\,\pm\,0.04\,\mathrm{mg}\) for particulate fractions. Column capacity was checked by assaying the first \(10\ \mathrm{ml}\) wash fraction for activity; none was detected. After each column was washed with \(10\ \mathrm{ml}\) of homogenization buffer, the bound PKC present in the samples was then eluted with \(7\ \mathrm{ml}\) of homogenization buffer containing \(100\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\). For investigating PKM activity in cytosolic fractions, the columns were subsequently eluted with \(5\ \mathrm{ml}\) of homogenization buffer containing \(300\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\). In preliminary experiments, cytosolic fractions of uterine arteries were applied to DEAE Bio-Gel A columns and fractions were collected throughout either a linear gradient of \(0{-}400\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\) or batch gradient (\(10\ \mathrm{ml}\), \(7\ \mathrm{ml}\), \(5\ \mathrm{ml}\)) of \(0\), \(100\), and \(300\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\). Column fractions were assayed for protein kinase activities in the presence of \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) and lipids (PKC activity) or in the presence of EGTA (defined as \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\)/lipid-independent kinase activity or PKM). The peak of PKC activity occurred at \(60\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\) during the linear gradient and the PKM eluted at \(140\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\).
Ninety-seven percent of the PKC activity eluted in the \(100\ \mathrm{mM}\) fraction during batch gradient collection and PKM was verified to be present in the \(300\ \mathrm{mM}\) fraction (Fig. 1). Variability between columns was less than \(5\%\) for PKC activity elution profiles. For quantifying PKC and PKM activities of uterine artery preparations, enzyme was routinely eluted at \(100\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\) (PKC) and \(300\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\) (PKM) in buffer; eluates were kept on ice for less than \(60\ \mathrm{min}\) before assay. Columns were used repeatedly, with regeneration carried out in situ by washing with \(0.01\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{NaOH}\) followed by deionized water and homogenization buffer. Background activity for a representative column (which was repeatedly used) is depicted in Figure 1. As shown, no PKC activity was present in the first \(10\) fractions prior to addition of \(100\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\) nor in the \(300\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\) fraction.
Protein kinase assay. DEAE Bio-Gel A column fractions were assayed for protein kinase activity as previously described [20]. The enzyme assay was based on the property of histone III-S to serve as substrate for activated PKC and undergo subsequent phosphorylation in the presence of \([\tau^{32}\mathrm{P}]\mathrm{ATP}\). PKC incubation mixture contained, in a final
volume of \(0.25\;\mathrm{ml}\), \(20\;\mathrm{mM}\) Tris-HCl \((\mathrm{pH}\,7.5)\), \(5\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{MgNO}_{3}\), \(200\;\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}\) histone type III-S, \(10\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\;[\pi-^{32}\mathrm{P}]\mathrm{ATP}\), and \(50\;\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of column eluate containing \(0.1{-}20\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\) protein. The assay solution was supplemented with either EGTA \((1\;\mathrm{mM})\) or \(\mathbf{CaCl}_{2}\) \((1\;\mathrm{mM})\) plus lipids. Phosphatidylserine \((40\;\upmu\mathrm{g/ml})\) and 1,2-dioleine \((4\;\upmu\mathrm{g/ml})\) were the standard lipids used. After incubation for \(6\;\mathrm{min}\) at \(30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the reaction mixture was stopped by transferring \(100\;\upmu\mathrm{l}\) onto filter paper and rapidly immersing the sample in \(10\%\) trichloroacetic acid (TCA). After extensive washing in \(10\%\) TCA, \({}^{32}\mathrm{P}\) incorporation into precipitated protein was determined by liquid scintillation spectroscopy. Radioactivity nonspecifically trapped on filters ("filter blank") was determined in each experiment and was subtracted from radioactivity present on reaction filters.
PKC activity present in the \(100\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{NaCl}\) fraction was defined as that seen in the presence of \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) plus lipids minus that seen in the presence of EGTA and no lipids. PKM activity was determined by assaying the high salt (300 \(\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{NaCl}\)) cytosolic fraction for incorporation of \({}^{32}\mathrm{P}\) into histone in the absence of calcium and lipids. Protein kinase C activity was linearly dependent on protein concentration \((1{-}15\;\upmu\mathrm{g})\) and time of incubation \((3{-}6\;\mathrm{min})\). For experiments reported in this study, \(500\;\mathrm{mg}\) of tissue was used and an assay incubation time of \(6\;\mathrm{min}\) was employed. Furthermore, when various amounts of tissue \((50{-}500\;\mathrm{mg})\) were homogenized, partially purified, and assayed, both cytosolic and particulate PKC activities were linearly related to the quantity of tissue used \((r=0.994\); \(r=0.997)\).
## Statistics
For the in vitro perfusion data, differences in uterine arterial response were statistically evaluated by split-plot analysis for a completely randomized design, with perfusion periods as the subplot, and differences in \({}^{45}\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake of arteries and steroid levels in gilts were analyzed by factorial analysis of variance [21]. Correlations between arterial \({}^{45}\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake, in vitro responsiveness, and plasma steroid concentrations were calculated throughout gestation. Differences in uterine arterial PKC and PKM activities were evaluated by factorial analysis of variance [20].
## RESULTS
## Study 1
The contractile response of the uterine artery to KCl, as well as its ability to take up extracellular \({}^{45}\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\), declined \((p<0.05)\) progressively from Day 20 through Day 80 of gestation, then remained low through Day 110 (Table 1). An association between uterine arterial contractility to KCl throughout gestation and its ability to take up extracellular \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) was suggested by the highly significant correlation between these two responses \((r=+0.97\); \(p<0.01)\). The responsiveness of the gastroepiploic artery to a KCl-induced contraction, as well as its uptake of extracellular \({}^{45}\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\), remained relatively constant from Day 20 to Day 110 of gestation, averaging \(42\pm5\;\mathrm{mm}\;\mathrm{Hg}\) and \(105\pm11\;\upmu\mathrm{mol/kg}\) arterial tissue, respectively.
contraction, as well as its uptake of extracellular \({}^{45}\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\), remained relatively constant from Day 20 to Day 110 of gestation, averaging \(42\pm5\;\mathrm{mm}\;\mathrm{Hg}\) and \(105\pm11\;\upmu\mathrm{mol/kg}\) arterial tissue, respectively.
Patterns of estradiol- \(17\beta\) and estrone in systemic blood were similar from Day 20 to Day 110 of gestation; thus these values were added for each sample and referred to as estrogens. Estrogen concentrations in systemic blood increased progressively from Day 20 \((11\pm2\;\mathrm{pg/ml})\) through Day 50 \((43\pm11\;\mathrm{pg/ml})\), Day 80 \((717\pm43)\), and Day 110 \((2648\pm236\;\mathrm{pg/ml})\). Progesterone concentrations in systemic blood remained relatively constant from Day 20 to Day 110 of gestation, averaging \(22\pm2\;\mathrm{ng/ml}\). Estrogen concentrations were negatively correlated with the contractile responses of the uterine arteries to KCl \((r=-0.57\); \(p<0.01)\) and with their ability to take up extracellular \({}^{45}\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) \((r=-0.93\); \(p<0.0001)\) during the course of gestation. No correlations were observed between progesterone and uterine arterial contractile responses or \({}^{45}\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake.
## Study 2
Uterine arterial cytosolic PKC activity remained relatively constant in luteal phase versus follicular phase of the estrous cycle (Fig. 2). Cytosolic PKC activity of uterine arteries during early (Days 36-46) gestation were similar to values observed during the estrous cycle, then declined significantly \((p<0.05)\) during mid- (Days 50-75) and late- (Days 80-95) gestation. Membrane-associated PKC activity remained low and relatively constant throughout the stages of the estrous cycle and gestation that were examined (Fig. 2). Redistribution of cytosolic PKC activity to the membrane compartment was not evident at any stage examined.
The catalytically active fragment PKM was found predominantly in cytosolic fractions prepared from uterine arteries of both nonpregnant and pregnant pigs (Fig. 2). Particulate fractions were analyzed for PKM activity by assaying the 300 \(\mathrm{mM}\) fraction and only a constant, low level of activity \((<5\;\mathrm{pmol/mg/min})\) was observed. Cytosolic PKM activity exhibited a small, nonsignificant increase from the luteal phase to the follicular phase of the estrous cycle, but had increased significantly \((p<0.05)\) over luteal phase values by late gestation (Days 80-95). Further, these changes in cytosolic PKM activity were negatively correlated \((r=-0.63\); \(p<0.01)\) with cytosolic PKC activity in the same animals.
## DISCUSSION
These data demonstrate that the uptake of extracellular \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) by uterine arterial tissue declines as estrogen levels increase with advancing gestation. The strong association between these events is suggested by the highly significant \((p<0.01)\) negative correlation \((r=-0.93)\) between \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake and circulating estrogen concentrations throughout gestation. This observation is in agreement with previously published data suggesting a role for estrogen in preventing specific uptake of extracellular \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) by vascular smooth muscle cells through PSC [7,8]. The progressive decrease \((p<0.01)\) in uterine arterial responsiveness to a depolarizing dose of KCl and the significant correlation between KCl-induced contraction and \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake \((r=0.97; p<0.01)\) further suggests a progressive inhibition of \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake through the PSC, with a resultant vasodilation as gestation advances in the pig. Further evidence for a local effect of gestation in preferentially vasodilating the uterine arterial vasculature is the consistent responsiveness of the gastroepiploic artery to KCl, as well as \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake, throughout gestation.
Several lines of evidence suggest that hydroxylated estrogen metabolites named catechol estrogens may be the active principles inducing the uterine arterial vasodilation observed during gestation. Stice et al. [7] observed that catechol estrogens, but not the parent estrogens, specifically block \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake through PSC in the pig uterine arterial
smooth muscle membrane. Catechol estrogens are a major group of naturally occurring estrogenic compounds formed by aromatic hydroxylation of precursor estrogens in mammalian tissues. Catechol estrogens are produced as early as Day 13 of gestation by the pig conceptus [22] and are known to be potent stimulators of uterine blood flow [23]. Synthesis of catechol estrogens from the parent estrogens has been shown to occur in the placenta [24] and locally within the uterine arteries [9]. Catechol estrogens are good candidates for local vasodilators during gestation because pig endometrial catechol-o-methyl transferase (COMT) activity declines with advancing gestation [25] and they are rapidly metabolized by COMT in blood [26].
There is now ample evidence suggesting that PKC plays an important role in maintaining isometric force in vascular smooth muscle [27]. Data from Silver et al. [28] illustrate that differences in PKC activity can exist in various vascular beds. In the present study, cytosolic PKC activity declined during late gestation, the period when the uterine artery was undergoing marked vasodilation. These data are in agreement with those of Silver et al. [28], who observed that PKC activity in the cytosol of the renal artery from normotensive rats is twofold less than that found in an age-and/or sex-matched hypertensive group. In further agreement with the present study, they reported that PKC activity in the membrane fraction remains constant across these groups. Protein kinase C has been shown to directly in
crease the entry of extracellular calcium via the PSC [29]. The importance to calcium influx through the PSC in maintaining PKC activity is consistent with studies demonstrating that the PSC agonist Bay K 8644 stimulates [30], whereas the PSC blocker nitrendipine inhibits [31], the sustained response to TPA. Thus, uterine arterial PKC activity may decline with the advancement of gestation as a result of the marked inhibition of calcium uptake through the PSC.
In the present study, the majority of PKC activity was found in the cytosolic rather than the particulate fraction of uterine arteries. This is a similar finding to that observed in human fetal adrenal glands [32], the corpus luteum [33], and ovine uterine and systemic arteries [15]. Further, unlike the particulate fraction, which did not change with biologic state (i.e., estrous cycle or gestation), the uterine arterial cytosolic PKC activity declined significantly during late gestation. The down-regulation of cytosolic PKC activity observed during late gestation may result from an enhanced proteolytic cleavage of PKC. This loss of cytosolic PKC activity may be associated with the elevation in cytosolic PKM as Kishimoto et al. [14] demonstrated using purified PKC and a calcium-activated neutral protease. In the present study, the cytosolic PKM activity was significantly increased by late gestation (Days 80-95) in pig uterine arteries and may suggest that the event of PKC-to-PKM conversion is linked to other events of late gestation such as the increases in uterine blood flow. Evidence for this hypothesis is the observed negative correlation \((p<0.01; r=-0.63)\) between cytosolic PKC and PKM for gilts in this study. Further, this decline in cytosolic PKC during gestation may be specific to the uterine vascular bed, since Magness et al. [15] observed significant declines in uterine arterial PKC but not systemic arterial PKC over the final one third of ovine pregnancy. Unlike the findings of Magness et al. [15] in the sheep, however, we failed to observe changes in uterine arterial PKC from the follicular phase versus the luteal phase of the estrous cycle in the pig. The reason for this discrepancy is not obvious but may be species related.
Although indirect, a role for progesterone in mediating the effects of estrogen can be hypothesized since uterine arterial cytosolic PKC activity only declines during late gestation (high estrogen and high progesterone) and not during the follicular phase (high estrogen and low progesterone) of the estrous cycle. Conversion of estrogens to their catechol forms appears to be mediated by a soluble uterine peroxidase [34], and recent observations from our laboratory suggest that a prolonged (7 days) exposure of the ovariectomized pig to both estrogen and progesterone are required to elevate soluble peroxidase activity in uterine arterial tissue [35]. As previously stated, catechol estrogens, but not the parent estrogens, inhibit \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake through the PSC [7,8]. Calcium uptake through the PSC has been shown to be necessary for PKC-induced sustained arterial contractions [35]. These data support those of Greiss and Anderson [36], who observed that progesterone administration restores uterine vascular sensitivity to estrogen-induced vasodilation after tachyphylaxis. Alternatively, the marked increase in estrogen concentrations seen at the end of gestation may provide additional substrate for conversion to catechol forms.
