These data are consistent with the hypothesis that the increasing local exposure of the uterine artery to placental estrogens results in the peroxidase-mediated conversion of the estrogens to their catechol forms, which inhibit the influx of calcium through the PSC. This inhibition of calcium influx may result in a down-regulation of PKC activity or its more rapid conversion to the cytosolic fragment PKM. Further, the concurrent elevation in uterine arterial compliance and PKM suggests that the proteolytic activation of PKC may be linked to gestation-induced changes in uterine arterial blood flow. Due to the descriptive nature of these data, however, it is possible that catechol estrogens and PKC represent independent regulatory pathways modulating uterine blood flow.
